gles. Let's see. If the next tile is B7, then B5-B6-B7 chow. If next tile is T6, then T4-T5-T6 chow. If next tile is W4, then W3-W4-W5 chow. If next tile is W6, then W5-W6-W7 chow. So there are multiple possibilities for forming chows. This seems better. The hand has potential to form multiple chows, so discarding B6 might be a good move to set up for multiple waits.\n\n5. Play F1: Discard F1. Remaining hand: W3, W5, W7, T4, T5, T7, B5, B6 (2), B8, F2, F3, J1. Pair is B6. Similar to before. Not as good as discarding B6.\n\n6. Play T5: Discard T5. Remaining hand: W3, W5, W7, T4, T7, B5, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, F3, J1. Now, T4 and T7 are left. If next tile is T5 or T6, can form chows. But T5 is discarded, so maybe safer.\n\n7. Play B5: Discard B5. Remaining hand: W3, W5, W7, T4, T5, T7, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, F3, J1. Now, Dots have B6 and B8. If next tile is B7, then B6-B7-B8 chow. If next tile is B5, but it's discarded. So maybe less options.\n\n8. Play F3: Discard F3. Remaining hand: W3, W5, W7, T4, T5, T7, B5, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, J1. Pair is B6. Similar to before.\n\n9. Play T7: Discard T7. Remaining hand: W3, W5, W7, T4, T5, B5, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, F3, J1. Now, T4 and T5 left. If next tile is T6, then T4-T5-T6 chow. If next tile is T7, but discarded.\n\n10. Play W7: Discard W7. Remaining hand: W3, W5, T4, T5, T7, B5, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, F3, J1. W3 and W5 left. If next tile is W4 or W6, can form chows.\n\n11. Play T4: Discard T4. Remaining hand: W3, W5, W7, T5, T7, B5, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, F3, J1. Now, T5 and T7 left. If next tile is T4 or T6, can form chows.\n\n12. Play W5: Discard W5. Remaining hand: W3, W7, T4, T5, T7, B5, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, F3, J1. W3 and W7 left. If next tile is W4 or W6, can form chows.\n\n13. Play J1: Discard J1. Remaining hand: W3, W5, W7, T4, T5, T7, B5, B6 (2), B8, F1, F2, F3. Pair is B6. Similar to before.\n\nLooking at all options, discarding B6 seems to leave a pair and allow for multiple possible chows in different suits. If I discard B6, the remaining hand has B5 and B8. If the next tile is B7, that's a chow. Similarly, in Bamboo, if the next tile is T6, completing T4-T5-T6. In Characters, if next tile is W4 or W6, completing W3-W4-W5 or W5-W6-W7. So this gives multiple waiting options, which is good for forming a winning hand. Also, having a concealed hand (since all melds are concealed) can give points for \"Fully Concealed Hand\" (4 points) and possibly others. \n\nAdditionally, the hand has a pair of B6, which is a terminal tile (B6 is not a terminal; terminals are 1 and 9). Wait, B6 is a middle tile. So the pair is not a terminal. So maybe not contributing to \"Outside Hand\" (which requires terminals or honors in every meld and pair). But the hand might have \"All Simples\" if all tiles are non-terminals and non-honors. Let's check: W3, W5, W7 (non-terminals), T4, T5, T7 (non-terminals), B5, B6, B8 (non-terminals), and the pair is B6. So yes, all are simples. \"All Simples\" gives 2 points. Also, \"Fully Concealed Hand\" gives 4 points. That's 6 points. But need at least 8 to win. So maybe need more.\n\nLooking for other patterns. Since the hand is concealed, maybe \"All Chows\" if all melds are chows. If the next tile allows forming four chows and a pair. For example, if I discard B6 and draw B7, then B5-B6-B7. Then, if I can form other chows. Let's see: Suppose I have B5, B6, B7. Then in Bamboo, if I have T4, T5, T6 (if I draw T6). And in Characters, W3, W4, W5 (if I draw W4). So if I can form multiple chows. But it's possible. If I can form four chows and a pair, that would be \"All Chows\" (2 points). Plus \"Fully Concealed Hand\" (4) and \"All Simples\" (2). Total 8 points. That would be enough to win.\n\nTherefore, discarding B6 gives multiple waiting options and the potential to form a winning hand with 8 points. Other discards might not offer as many possibilities. So the best move is to play B6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing B6 allows forming a concealed hand with potential for multiple chows and a pair, maximizing scoring opportunities for \"Fully Concealed Hand\" (4 points), \"All Simples\" (2 points), and possibly \"All Chows\" (2 points), totaling 8 points to meet the minimum requirement.\nReason: Discarding B6 preserves a concealed hand with a pair and sets up multiple possible chows in different suits, increasing the chance to form a winning hand with sufficient points.\nAnswer: Play B6"}
